distort — encrypt or decrypt a stream with authenticated encryption

distort seals a stream of any length with XChaCha20-Poly1305 (libsodium's
crypto_secretstream) in 64 KiB chunks, so memory use is constant.  Every
chunk is authenticated: decryption fails if the stream was reordered,
truncated, extended or sealed with another key.

Building and installing
=======================

distort needs a C compiler and libsodium (Alpine: libsodium-dev;
Debian: libsodium-dev; Fedora: libsodium-devel; macOS: brew install libsodium).
From a release tarball:

    ./configure
    make
    make check          # optional
    sudo make install

For a self-contained binary: ./configure --enable-static-binary (fully static on Linux; libsodium linked in statically on macOS).

From a source checkout, run ./bootstrap first (needs autoconf, automake,
help2man and git).  See ./configure --help for the usual options such as
--prefix.

Usage
=====

Make a key: 32 random bytes, stored raw (or as 64 hex digits).

    head -c 32 /dev/urandom > key.key
    chmod 600 key.key

Encrypt and decrypt files or pipes:

    distort encrypt --key-file key.key file.bin > file.bin.enc
    distort decrypt --key-file key.key file.bin.enc > file.bin
    tar cz dir | distort encrypt -k key.key > dir.tgz.enc

decrypt writes each chunk only after verifying it.  If it fails, the output
so far is authentic but incomplete: trust the data only when the exit
status is 0.  See distort --help and man distort.

